From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 17:30:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GPU: D3D12 presents a transparent window through
 DirectComposition

A flip-model swapchain created for an HWND ignores alpha, so the D3D12
driver refused SDL_WINDOW_TRANSPARENT windows. On desktop Windows it now
creates the swapchain with CreateSwapChainForComposition instead
(premultiplied alpha, stretch scaling, sequential flip, an explicit size)
and shows it as the content of a DirectComposition visual, the root of a
topmost target for the window. DWM then composites the swapchain's alpha
over whatever is behind the window: the desktop, or a system backdrop
(Mica, Acrylic) the application asked DWM for.

- dcomp.dll is loaded with the first transparent window. dcomp.h is
  C++-only, so the three interfaces' methods used here are declared by
  their vtable slots.
- The DirectComposition device, target and visual live as long as the
  window is claimed: a window has only one topmost target. Recreating the
  swapchain (new present mode, composition or frames in flight) sets the
  new one as the visual's content.
- A composition swapchain has no window to take its size from, so a
  resize passes the window's pixel size to ResizeBuffers.
- DWM composites it like any other content, so it never tears:
  DXGI_SWAP_CHAIN_FLAG_ALLOW_TEARING and DXGI_PRESENT_ALLOW_TEARING are
  left off for it.
- Xbox and DXVK builds have no DirectComposition and still refuse a
  transparent window.

Untested on hardware so far; compiled for x86, x64 and arm64 Windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
